  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 AM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nA minor upper level disturbance will approach the central high\nplains today. Surface based t-storms will develop well to the\nnorth and west of this afternoon and then progress southward in\na weakened state into west central Kansas by midnight. The \nstorms could contain wind gusts of 50-60 mph and small hail \ngiven the linear and outflow dominated storm nature by the time\nstorms arrive. Some locations could receive up to a half inch \nor more of rain; but totals in general will be highly variable \nas is normally the case in late summer. The best chance for \nsignificant rains are west of a line from WaKeeney to Dodge \nCity. Expect highs in the lower to mid 90s today. Chances for \nrain Thursday night are low in the wake of Wednesday night's \nstabilizing storm cluster that will tend to have a slight \ncooling effect.\n\nUpper level ridging will slowly develop across the high plains\nby Friday and into the weekend. However, a shortwave trough\ntraversing the northern periphery of the ridge is expected to\npush a weak cold front into western Kansas by Sunday. Hot\ntemperatures near 100 degrees are forecast along and south of\nthe front for Saturday and Sunday. There are small chances for\nt-storms along the stalled front by Sunday evening, mainly \nacross west central Kansas and along I-70. The front will not \nmake it very far south next week given the upper level ridge \nfrom Texas eastward. Therefore, hot weather will persist through\nmid next week despite the presence of a weakening frontal \nboundary. T-storms can't be ruled out on any day given that \nwestern Kansas will be on the southern periphery of the mid to \nhigh level westerlies; but widespread rains are unlikely. The \ngrand ensemble indicates only 10-20% daily chances for >.25\" of \nrain.",
